WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.440
Hi it's Michelle from Lab Muffin Beauty
Science, skincare and oolong tea addict

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.099
Today I'm going to be talking about one
of the questions I get asked really

00:00:08.099 --> 00:00:12.599
really often: is chemical or physical
sunscreen better? I covered this topic

00:00:12.599 --> 00:00:17.039
quite a bit in my sunscreen and makeup
video, but I wanted to go more in depth

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:21.090
with it with this video, because so many
people have been asking me about it. If

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:25.050
you're into skincare and you have
questions about sunscreen I highly

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.369
recommend going to that video and giving
it a watch because in that video - apart

00:00:29.369 --> 00:00:33.930
from the fact that I think it's awesome
because I made the video obviously - I've

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:37.890
also put a lot of answers to the common
questions I get about sunscreen in there

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.210
and it isn't just about makeup and
sunscreen, it's also about wearing daily

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:46.430
sunscreen in general, and so the answers
to your questions might also be in there.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:52.379
So back to this question: is chemical or
physical sunscreen better? There are tons

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:56.250
of misconceptions online and there's
lots and lots of myths floating around

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:04.890
around this topic so I'm going to try to
cover all of these.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.670
Sunscreens stop UV light from getting
to your skin and damaging it which can

00:01:09.670 --> 00:01:14.050
lead to skin cancer and aging. Sunscreen
ingredients are often divided into

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:18.580
physical and chemical, which aren't great
names because hopefully I don't have to

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:22.090
explain to you that everything is a
chemical whether it's an ingredient

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:28.600
that's from nature or man-made. Water, DNA,
oxygen - all of these are chemicals. So

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.010
both chemical and physical sunscreens
are chemicals. Most people say that

00:01:33.010 --> 00:01:37.479
physical sunscreens are zinc oxide and
titanium dioxide while chemical

00:01:37.479 --> 00:01:42.310
sunscreens are everything else, so for
example oxybenzone avobenzone Tinosorb

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:47.649
S and M, octocrylene and so on. The
original idea was that chemical and

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:51.610
physical sunscreens work in different
ways, but now we know that they actually

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:56.380
don't. The idea was that in theory
chemical sunscreens would absorb UV

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.190
light and turn it into heat via their
chemical structures, while physical

00:02:00.190 --> 00:02:04.210
sunscreens scatter and reflect UV away
from your skin due to their physical

00:02:04.210 --> 00:02:09.940
particle size. But in reality there's a
lot of overlap. Tinosorb M has a

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.959
structure that looks like a lot of
chemical sunscreens so it absorbs UV and

00:02:13.959 --> 00:02:17.590
turns it into heat but it's also made
into particles that can scatter and

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:22.410
reflect some of the UV light. That makes
Tinosorb M both physical and chemical.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:26.980
Even the ingredients that most people
refer to as physical sunscreens zinc

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:32.080
oxide and titanium dioxide mostly absorb
UV light and turn it into heat. They only

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:37.360
scatter and reflect about 5% of incoming
UV. A better way of describing the two

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:41.950
different types of sunscreen ingredients
is organic and inorganic, so organic

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:46.000
sunscreens are those that are based on a
carbon chain structure while inorganic

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.650
sunscreens are minerals or ionic
compounds. So Tinosorb M would count as

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:54.910
an organic sunscreen while zinc oxide
and titanium dioxide would be inorganic.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:59.200
So now that we know what we're dealing
with let's talk about the advantages and

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:05.850
disadvantages of the two types of
sunscreen.

00:03:05.850 --> 00:03:10.390
The biggest advantage of organic or
chemical sunscreens is that you can get

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:14.950
a much higher protection level. You can
get over SPF 50 with both organic and

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:19.990
inorganic sunscreens but for longer
wavelengths of UVA called UVA1 there

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:24.490
are a lot less options. In the US
there's only really three options for

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:28.960
blocking these longer wavelengths of UVA.
The main two are the physical /

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.970
inorganic sunscreen zinc oxide, and the
organic sunscreen avobenzone. There's

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:38.710
also ecamsule or Mexoryl SX which is
patented by L'Oreal so it's only

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:42.850
available in a few sunscreens. In other
countries there are few more options for

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:46.600
UVA1 protection but they're all
chemical or organic sunscreen

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:52.690
ingredients, so for example Tinosorbs
S&amp;M, Uvinul A Plus and Mexoryl XL

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:56.830
It's impossible to say for sure how much
protection a sunscreen gives without

00:03:56.830 --> 00:04:00.610
testing it but the highest UVA
protection that I've seen for inorganic

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:06.670
sunscreens is about a UVAPF of around
20. The highest UVAPF values that you'll

00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:13.120
see are around UVAPF 40 which is always
for chemical or organic sunscreens. So if

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:17.620
your skin is particularly prone to
pigmentation or aging then an organic

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:25.649
sunscreen or chemical sunscreen gives
you better options.

00:04:25.670 --> 00:04:29.850
Another big advantage of organic
sunscreens is that they tend to give you

00:04:29.850 --> 00:04:35.340
less white cast. Inorganic sunscreens
particularly titanium dioxide tends to

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:40.230
make you look quite white especially if
your skin is quite dark, so for example

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:44.790
my skin's around NC 25 in MAC
foundation shades and with a lot of

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:50.460
physical sunscreens I look weirdly white.
This isn't really surprising because

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.200
zinc oxide and titanium dioxide, the
mineral sunscreens, are actually used to

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.710
make white paint. To make them more
transparent they can be ground up into

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:03.030
smaller particles such as microparticles and nanoparticles but it can

00:05:03.030 --> 00:05:07.110
still be too light for some people, and
because sunscreen protection depends on

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:10.590
how much of the ingredients you have on
your face, you want to go with more

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:15.300
sunscreen rather than less sunscreen, and
so, if a sunscreen is making you go white

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:21.080
you're going to want to put on less of
it, and so you'll get less protection.

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:28.830
Sunscreens tend to have really tricky
textures. Organic sunscreens tend to be

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:33.000
greasier while inorganic sunscreens
tend to be thicker and depending on your

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:37.530
skin type and skin conditions one type
might work better for you. I personally

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:41.190
prefer organic sunscreens because I have
quite a few that feel like moisturizer

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:44.970
and I've actually had an inorganic
sunscreen break me out within a few

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.910
hours of applying it.
There are also some combination

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:51.530
sunscreens which contain both types.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:59.290
Here's where inorganic sunscreens really
shine. Inorganic sunscreens tend to

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:03.910
cause fewer allergic and irritant
reactions. Like I mentioned before in the

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:07.660
US there are only really three
ingredients that protect against UVA1:

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:11.320
zinc oxide, avobenzone and Mexoryl
SX

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:15.850
Mexoryl SX is only in a few sunscreens
and so most of the time you only have

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:20.290
those two options: zinc oxide and avobenzone. Avobenzone can be really

00:06:20.290 --> 00:06:24.370
irritating if it's at over three percent
concentration and it can cause allergies

00:06:24.370 --> 00:06:29.320
if your skin is sensitive so if you do
have sensitive skin or anavobenzone

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:33.970
allergy then zinc oxide is going to be
your best option. However if you're

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:38.110
elsewhere in the world or if you have
access to sunscreens outside of the US

00:06:38.110 --> 00:06:43.330
then you might have access to the newer
UVA protection filters such as Tinosorb

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.950
S&amp;M and Uvinul A Plus and these
protect against longer UVA1

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:52.840
wavelengths without being particularly
allergenic or irritating. There are a few

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:56.410
other sunscreen ingredients that tend to
cause allergic or irritating reactions

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:00.400
and these are all chemical organic
sunscreens. Not all of them will cause

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.660
reactions but these ones are the ones
that tend to be the culprits: octocrylene,

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:12.580
oxybenzone, PABA, padimate O
and

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:17.310
enzacamene

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:23.960
So now that we've covered the pros and
cons of organic or chemical sunscreens

00:07:23.960 --> 00:07:28.250
and inorganic or physical sunscreens,
let's talk about some common myths about

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:32.510
these sunscreen ingredients that don't
really make a big difference. I'm sort of

00:07:32.510 --> 00:07:36.620
sick of saying organic or chemical and
inorganic or physical sunscreen each

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:41.000
time, so even though organic and
inorganic are the more correct options

00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:45.140
I'm going to say chemical and physical
because that's what 90% of the stuff

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:49.640
online says and so even though it's less
correct it's more popular so I'm going

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:57.710
to go with that. Sometimes you'll see
advice that says you need to apply a

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:02.210
chemical sunscreen on bare skin but
physical sunscreens go on top last over

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:05.690
your makeup. You might also see that
chemical sunscreens need to be applied

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:09.710
20 minutes before you go outside so it
has time to activate but physical

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:13.190
sunscreens work immediately.
Both of these myths are based on the

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:17.120
idea that chemical sunscreens need to
absorb into skin and bind before they

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:22.310
start to work and both of these myths
are false. All sunscreen ingredients work

00:08:22.310 --> 00:08:26.210
immediately straight out of the bottle.
Here's the demonstration I made for the

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:30.440
sunscreen and makeup video. I've applied
chemical and physical sunscreens to this

00:08:30.440 --> 00:08:34.580
piece of paper along with a moisturizer
that doesn't have sunscreen in it and

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.690
I'm shining a UV light on it. The dark
color is where the sunscreen is

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:43.729
absorbing UV. You can see that for both
sunscreens they're working immediately

00:08:43.729 --> 00:08:47.810
without any wait time. You should still
apply some all sunscreens a little bit

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:51.710
before you go outside so that it has
time to form a continuous film and dry

00:08:51.710 --> 00:08:58.450
down so it won't move as much, but you
can apply all sunscreens in the same way

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.990
The next myth is that physical
sunscreens are better because they're

00:09:04.990 --> 00:09:09.490
natural. Firstly natural things aren't
better than synthetic man-made things.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:13.810
I've made a whole video on this which
I've linked in the caption. Secondly

00:09:13.810 --> 00:09:18.670
physical sunscreens aren't even really
that natural. Firstly they need to be

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:22.209
processed to get rid of toxic
contaminants and this requires heating

00:09:22.209 --> 00:09:26.230
them up to really really unnaturally
high temperatures plus they need to be

00:09:26.230 --> 00:09:29.920
coated in synthetic chemicals to stop
them from being photocatalytic which

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.089
means they've reactants unlike to
produce free radicals if you opt for the

00:09:34.089 --> 00:09:37.990
uncoated version then it tends to clump
up on your skin and in the bottle and

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:47.380
you get patchy protection. The next myth
is that once UV is converted to heat by

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:52.120
a chemical sunscreen that heat will then
damage your skin. But the amount of heat

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:57.190
that's produced is really imperceptibly
tiny plus as I mentioned earlier

00:09:57.190 --> 00:10:02.770
physical sunscreens actually absorb 95% of
UV and turn that into heat so there's

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:06.250
only really a five percent difference
between a chemical sunscreen and a

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:12.279
physical sunscreen in terms of heat.

00:10:12.279 --> 00:10:16.640
The myth that you need to reapply
chemical sunscreens but not physical

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:20.959
sunscreens is based on the idea that
chemical sunscreens aren't photostable

00:10:20.959 --> 00:10:24.890
which means that they break down after
absorbing too much UV and you'll need to

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:29.390
replace the chemical sunscreen that's
been used up by the UV. This used to be

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:33.709
true for older sunscreens but these days
there are lots of sunscreens available

00:10:33.709 --> 00:10:38.060
that are photostable. The most
photo unstable combination is

00:10:38.060 --> 00:10:43.130
avobenzone and octinoxate and so you should
reapply that every two hours once you've

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:47.330
been in the sun. However you should
really be applying all sunscreens for

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:52.010
every two hours of sun exposure and
really two hours of total time even

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.910
without sun exposure the main reason
that it's recommended that you reapply

00:10:55.910 --> 00:11:00.649
sunscreen isn't because of photoinstability it's because the sunscreen

00:11:00.649 --> 00:11:04.279
film on your skin tends to shift around
and off your skin as you move around

00:11:04.279 --> 00:11:08.000
throughout the day and so you need to
reapply to get an even layer that will

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.930
give you even protection again so if
you're getting a lot of sun exposure if

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:15.529
you're in direct sun if you're outdoors
if you're playing sports you should be

00:11:15.529 --> 00:11:19.430
reapplying your sunscreen every two
hours or even more if you're sweating

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:23.899
heavily or if you're swimming however
studies on daily sunscreen use found

00:11:23.899 --> 00:11:28.190
significant benefits when you apply
sunscreen only once a day and so if

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:31.490
you're just using sunscreen under your
makeup and you're not doing a lot of

00:11:31.490 --> 00:11:35.390
exercise and you're not getting a lot of
sun exposure you can reapply less

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:41.660
frequently and still get lots of
benefits

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:46.679
It's sort of true that you can use less
of a physical sunscreen you need to use

00:11:46.679 --> 00:11:50.759
the same weight of sunscreen but
physical sunscreens tend to be denser

00:11:50.759 --> 00:11:55.499
than chemical sunscreens and so on
average you can use around 20% or 1/5

00:11:55.499 --> 00:12:00.389
less of a physical sunscreen by volume
but not by weight so even though you're

00:12:00.389 --> 00:12:09.119
using a small volume you still end up
with the same weight of sunscreen. This

00:12:09.119 --> 00:12:12.989
is a big talking point and it doesn't
help that organic sunscreens are called

00:12:12.989 --> 00:12:16.529
chemical sunscreens which makes them
sound a lot scarier than physical

00:12:16.529 --> 00:12:19.829
sunscreens but it's important to
remember that there are a lot of

00:12:19.829 --> 00:12:24.029
different chemical sunscreens and they
all have different effects. Some of them

00:12:24.029 --> 00:12:27.600
get into your skin and into your body
much more easily while some of them

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:31.619
can't get into your skin much at all
some of them absorb a lot more UV more

00:12:31.619 --> 00:12:35.009
efficiently than other ones and so you
end up having less of them in each

00:12:35.009 --> 00:12:39.660
sunscreen and they all have different
hormonal effects. Another thing that you

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.929
should be aware of is that you'll often
hear that ingredients have been found in

00:12:42.929 --> 00:12:47.040
breast milk and urine which again sounds
really scary but there's a few issues

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.389
with that. Firstly we have really
sophisticated machines and analytical

00:12:51.389 --> 00:12:54.959
techniques that can detect really tiny
amounts of chemicals in all sorts of

00:12:54.959 --> 00:12:59.879
things and so finding something in a
particular substance doesn't mean it's

00:12:59.879 --> 00:13:03.509
in a high enough concentration to
actually do anything and it's important

00:13:03.509 --> 00:13:07.079
to remember that that ingredient has to
be in a high enough concentration to do

00:13:07.079 --> 00:13:11.519
something to be worth worrying about
for example there's tons of water in

00:13:11.519 --> 00:13:15.149
breast milk and urine but that's
completely fine because water is safe.

00:13:15.149 --> 00:13:19.230
The chemical sunscreen with the
strongest hormonal effect is oxybenzone

00:13:19.230 --> 00:13:24.029
and that's according to in vitro and
animal studies. But these studies found

00:13:24.029 --> 00:13:27.389
that you would need much higher
concentrations than that you'd use in

00:13:27.389 --> 00:13:31.230
everyday life. One dermatology paper
calculated that you would need to use

00:13:31.230 --> 00:13:37.649
oxybenzone sunscreen continuously for
277 years to get a hormonal effect. Other

00:13:37.649 --> 00:13:41.040
sunscreens that have had hormonal
effects in studies are enzacamene,

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:44.850
Padimate O, octinoxate and homosalate. Again,

00:13:44.850 --> 00:13:49.139
The amounts used in these studies would
convert to way way larger concentrations

00:13:49.139 --> 00:13:52.800
than the amounts that we would be
exposed to through sunscreen. One

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.310
paper called these concentrations
clinically unobtainable which means

00:13:56.310 --> 00:14:00.810
you're not going to see them in normal
people and then far far less likely to

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:04.170
have a noticeable effect than
oxybenzone which is still considered

00:14:04.170 --> 00:14:08.250
safe. Everyone has different standards of
risk so it's up to you if you want to

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:11.490
avoid these ingredients even though
they're all currently considered safe

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:15.750
but if you had to avoid one, oxybenzone
would probably be the one that you

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:25.620
should avoid. The physical sunscreens
zinc oxide and titanium dioxide have to

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:28.890
be ground up into extremely tiny
particles to avoid looking like white

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:33.510
facepaint and as I mentioned before they
are actually used as pigments in white

00:14:33.510 --> 00:14:38.130
paint. The particles are often either
microparticles which are 100 to

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:42.570
2500 nanometers
across or even smaller nanoparticles

00:14:42.570 --> 00:14:46.950
which are less than 100 nanometers
in length. Nanoparticles can potentially

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:51.300
make reactions happen in your body and
it's a particular concern in UV light

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:55.470
but the studies so far have found that
nanoparticles don't really get very deep

00:14:55.470 --> 00:15:00.510
into your skin, only into the dead layers
of the stratum corneum. It's possible

00:15:00.510 --> 00:15:04.410
that they can penetrate further if you
put the sunscreen on broken skin but

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:10.950
again they currently considered safe

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:16.270
Both chemical and physical sunscreens
have been found to react in UV light to

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:20.050
produce highly reactive free radicals
which are substances that have an

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:24.850
unpaired electron which makes them able
to attack and react with lots of things

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:29.680
in your bodies such as DNA lipids and
cells. This is a concern because free

00:15:29.680 --> 00:15:33.940
radical damage is the same way that UVA
causes aging while this sounds very

00:15:33.940 --> 00:15:38.440
scary these studies were generally
performed in vitro with cell lines while

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:41.890
studies on humans have found that
sunscreens will reduce skin cancer and

00:15:41.890 --> 00:15:46.210
aging. It's also worth remembering that
these reactions only really happen when

00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:50.110
UV hits the sunscreen and so they
generally only occur within the

00:15:50.110 --> 00:16:00.220
sunscreen film or in the top dead layers
of your skin. So after all of that here's

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:03.700
The moral of the story:
use whatever sunscreen works best for

00:16:03.700 --> 00:16:08.050
your skin type whether it's oily or dry
skin concerns like hyperpigmentation and

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:12.790
acne and your budget. Ideally what you
want is a sunscreen that has high

00:16:12.790 --> 00:16:17.860
protection so SPF 30 or above and
broad-spectrum protection so it protects

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:21.730
you against the longer wavelengths of
UVA as well as the shorter wavelengths

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:27.010
that are measured by SPF

00:16:27.790 --> 00:16:32.410
I personally like using sunscreens with
newer organic filters because I'm really

00:16:32.410 --> 00:16:36.210
prone to hyperpigmentation my dad is
basically one giant mole and so

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:41.350
genetically I'm screwed so what I want
is high UVA protection and I want it to

